            <ArticleTitle>A negative finding in an exercise test is reliable among elderly people: a follow-up study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>159-64</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Coronary heart disease (CHD) is very common among elderly people.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To specify the diagnostic value of bicycle exercise tests conducted for elderly patients by trained general practitioners in primary health care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed a 2-year follow-up study at the Kangasala Health Centre, Finland. The study population comprised all patients at least 60 years old (n = 311) suspected of having CHD without prior diagnosis who were examined by an exercise test carried out by trained general practitioners during a period of 3 years. Specificity and sensitivity as well as positive and negative predictive values of the tests were calculated based on whether or not the participants had a diagnosis of CHD by the end of the follow-up period. The numbers of patients referred for coronary angiography and bypass operation were registered. For analysis, the patients were divided into two age categories: 60-69 years and &gt;or=70 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A negative finding in the test proved very reliable: CHD manifested in only 3% of these cases in both age groups. The specificity of the test was 72% in the group 60-69 years and 66% in the group &gt;or=70 years; the sensitivity values were 81 and 67%, respectively. The positive predictive values were 26 and 12%, the negative predictive value was 97% in both age groups. 1 in 4 patients yielding a positive finding eventually underwent coronary angiography and 1 in 6 patients coronary bypass surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Exercise tests may predict the clinical outcome of CHD among elderly patients. A negative finding in an exercise test is very reliable.</AbstractText>
